ompanion instruction file — AAIF format (~15 tools)\r\n154: ├── scripts/          # Functional code + run_pipeline.py (multi-script) + run_evals.py\r\n155: ├── references/       # Detailed documentation (loaded on demand)\r\n156: ├── assets/           # Templates, schemas, data files\r\n157: ├── evals/            # Bundled eval spec: binary checks + golden cases\r\n158: ├── install.sh        # Cross-platform auto-detect installer\r\n159: └── README.md         # Multi-platform installation instructions\r\n160: ```\r\n161: \r\n162: Once installed, anyone on any platform types `/skill-name` and the skill activates — exactly like `/agent-skill-creator` or `/clarity`. The generated skill is a first-class citizen, not a second-class output.\r\n163: \r\n164: ## Core Workflow\r\n165: \r\n166: ### Phase 1: Discovery\r\n167: \r\n168: Research available APIs and data sources for the user's domain. Compare options by cost, rate limits, data quality, and documentation. **Decide** which API to use with justification.\r\n169: \r\n170: See `references/pipeline-phases.md` for detailed Phase 1 instructions.\r\n171: \r\n172: ### Phase 2: Design\r\n173: \r\n174: Define 4-6 priority analyses covering 80% of use cases. For each: name, objective, inputs, outputs, methodology. Always include a comprehensive report function.\r\n175: \r\n176: See `references/pipeline-phases.md` for detailed Phase 2 instructions.\r\n177: \r\n178: **Phase 2 includes an Artifact Opportunity Assessment step.** After the\r\n179: domain is identified, the creator runs `scripts/artifact_detector.py` on\r\n180: the description. If the output is visualizable (time series, comparison,\r\n181: KPIs, or structured rows), one of four bundled React templates is inlined\r\n182: into the generated SKILL.md along with Claude's artifact emission\r\n183: protocol. The artifact renders in Claude environments; in other hosts the\r\n184: component source appears as fenced code and the markdown analysis is\r\n185: unchanged. See `references/phase2-artifact-assessment.md` for details.\r\n186: \r\n187: **Override flags** — parse the user's prompt for these tokens BEFORE calling the detector:\r\n188: - `--no-artifact` anywhere in the user's prompt: skip the assessment entirely and generate the skill without any artifact template, exactly as v4 did. Strip the token from the prompt before passing it to Phase 1.\r\n189: - `--artifact <name>` (where `<name>` is `line-chart`, `bar-chart`, `kpi-cards`, or `data-table`): skip the detector and inline the named template directly. If `<name>` is not one of the four valid names, reject with an error listing the four valid values and stop. Strip the flag and value from the prompt before passing it to Phase 1.\r\n190: - `--no-eval` anywhere in the user's prompt: skip the Eval Criteria Definition step (below); the generated skill carries no `evals/` directory and no `run_evals.py`. Strip the token from the prompt before passing it to Phase 1.\r\n191: \r\n192: When neither flag is present, call the detector and let it decide.\r\n193: \r\n194: **Phase 2 also includes an Eval Criteria Definition step.** After the use\r\n195: cases are defined, derive the skill's loss function: 3–6 binary checks (each\r\n196: graded by a shell `command` or flagged `llm-judge`) plus at least 3 golden\r\n197: cases — seeded from the user's artifacts when available, otherwise synthesized\r\n198: as input-only `pending-first-green` cases. Present them for a one-word\r\n199: thumbs-up. The spec is written in Phase 5 to `evals/<name>.eval.md` and ships\r\n200: with the skill as an instant regression test, formatted so\r\n201: `autoresearch-universal` consumes it directly (its rule 18). Eval generation is\r\n202: **on by default**; `--no-eval` opts out. See\r\n203: `references/phase2-eval-assessment.md` for criteria rules, the golden-case\r\n204: strategy, the JSON spec format, and the optimize handoff.\r\n205: \r\n206: ### Phase 3: Architecture\r\n207: \r\n208: Structure the skill using the Agent Skills Open Standard:\r\n209: \r\n210: - **Simple Skill**: Single SKILL.md + scripts + references + assets\r\n211: - **Complex Suite**: Multiple component skills with shared resources\r\n212: \r\n213: **Decision criteria**: Number of workflows, code complexity, maintenance needs.\r\n214: \r\n215: See `references/architecture-guide.md` for decision logic and directory structures.\r\n216: \r\n217: ### Phase 4: Detection\r\n218: \r\n219: Generate a description (<=1024 chars) with domain keywords for agent discovery. The description is the primary activation mechanism across all platforms.\r\n220: \r\n221: See `references/pipeline-phases.md` for detailed Phase 4 instructions.\r\n222: \r\n223: ### Phase 5: Implementation\r\n224: \r\n225: Create all files in this order:\r\n226: \r\n227: 1. Create directory structure\r\n228: 2. Write **SKILL.md** — starts with `# /skill-name`, includes trigger section with invocation examples, spec-compliant frontmatter\r\n229: 3. Write **AGENTS.md** — companion instruction file for maximum cross-tool reach (~15 tools read AGENTS.md). Contains skill purpose, activation triggers, usage instructions, and a reference to SKILL.md for full details. Follows the AAIF-governed AGENTS.md format\r\n230: 4. Implement Python scripts (functional, no placeholders, no TODOs). **For a multi-script pipeline**, also emit a single `scripts/run_pipeline.py` orchestrator that runs the steps in order and wires output→input **in code** — so the agent runs one command instead of sequencing steps from prose. Skip for genuinely interactive/branching skills. See `references/phase5-orchestration.md`\r\n231: 5. Write references (detailed documentation the skill loads on demand)\r\n232: 6. Write assets (templates, configs)\r\n233: 7. **Emit the eval spec** (skip if `--no-eval`): write `evals/<name>.eval.md` (the binary checks + golden cases derived in Phase 2) and copy `scripts/run_evals_template.py` → the generated skill's `scripts/run_evals.py`. See `references/phase2-eval-assessment.md`\r\n234: 8. Generate `install.sh` from `scripts/install-template.sh` (replace `{{SKILL_NAME}}` with actual name, `chmod +x`)\r\n235: 9. Write `README.md` (multi-platform install instructions showing `git clone` to each tool's **native** path)\r\n236: 10. Run **validation** against the official spec, **security scan** for hardcoded keys and injection patterns, **`python3 <skill>/scripts/check_pipeline.py <skill>`** (no compile or undeclared-dependency errors), and — if an eval spec was emitted — `python3 <skill>/scripts/run_evals.py --validate` (must report `VALID`)\r\n237: 11. **Auto-install on the current platform** (see below)\r\n238: 12. Report results to user with clear next steps, including the eval/optimize one-liner from `references/phase2-eval-assessment.md`\r\n239: \r\n240: ### Auto-Install After Creation\r\n241: \r\n242: After the skill passes validation and security scan, install it immediately on the user's current platform. Do not ask the user to run `install.sh` manually — you are already running inside their environment and can detect their platform.\r\n243: \r\n244: **Detection logic** (check in order, install to each tool's **native** path):\r\n245: \r\n246: ```\r\n247: ~/.claude/              exists → Claude Code         → ~/.claude/skills/\r\n248: ~/.copilot/             exists → GitHub Copilot CLI  → ~/.copilot/skills/\r\n249: .github/                exists → VS Code Copilot     → .github/skills/ (project)\r\n250: .cursor/                exists → Cursor              → .cursor/skills/ (project only, no global path)\r\n251: ~/.codeium/windsurf/    exists → Windsurf            → ~/.codeium/windsurf/skills/ (global) + format adapt\r\n252: .windsurf/              exists → Windsurf            → .windsurf/rules/ (project) + format adapt\r\n253: .clinerules/ or ~/.cline/ exists → Cline             → .clinerules/skills/ or ~/.cline/skills/\r\n254: ~/.gemini/              exists → Gemini CLI          → ~/.gemini/skills/\r\n255: .kiro/                  exists → Kiro                → .kiro/skills/ (project)\r\n256: .trae/                  exists → Trae                → .trae/rules/ + format adapt (plain .md)\r\n257: .roo/                   exists → Roo Code            → .roo/skills/\r\n258: ~/.config/goose/        exists → Goose               → ~/.config/goose/skills/\r\n259: ~/.config/opencode/     exists → OpenCode            → ~/.config/opencode/skills/\r\n260: ~/.agents/              exists → Universal           → ~/.agents/skills/\r\n261: ```\r\n262: \r\n263: After installing to the native path, **also create a symlink at `~/.agents/skills/`** so the skill is discoverable by tools reading the universal path (Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, OpenCode, Goose, Cline, Roo Code).\r\n264: \r\n265: **Format adaptation**: For Tier 2 platforms (Cursor, Windsurf, Trae), also generate the native format alongside SKILL.md:\r\n266: - **Cursor**: Generate `.mdc` file with `alwaysApply: true` and description from frontmatter\r\n267: - **Windsurf**: Generate plain `.md` rule, respect 6,000 char per-file limit\r\n268: - **Trae**: Generate plain `.md` rule with `type: Always` frontmatter\r\n269: \r\n270: **Install action**: Copy or symlink the generated skill directory into the platform's native skill path:\r\n271: \r\n272: ```bash\r\n273: # Claude Code (user-level):\r\n274: cp -R ./sales-report-skill ~/.claude/skills/sales-report-skill\r\n275: \r\n276: # GitHub Copilot (user-level — Copilot's own path, not Claude's):\r\n277: cp -R ./sales-report-skill ~/.copilot/skills/sales-report-skill\r\n278: \r\n279: # GitHub Copilot (project-level):\r\n280: cp -R ./sales-report-skill .github/skills/sales-report-skill\r\n281: \r\n282: # Cursor (project-level ONLY — no global path exists):\r\n283: cp -R ./sales-report-skill .cursor/skills/sales-report-skill\r\n284: \r\n285: # Gemini CLI (native path):\r\n286: cp -R ./sales-report-skill ~/.gemini/skills/sales-report-skill\r\n287: ```\r\n288: \r\n289: **After installing, tell the user exactly what to do next:**\r\n290: \r\n291: ```\r\n292: Skill installed successfully.\r\n293: \r\n294: To use it, open a new session and type:\r\n295: \r\n296:   /sales-report-skill Generate the weekly report for the West region\r\n297: \r\n298: The skill is installed at: ~/.claude/skills/sales-report-skill\r\n299: ```\r\n300: \r\n301: If you cannot detect the platform, show the user how to run the install manually:\r\n302: \r\n303: ```\r\n304: I couldn't auto-detect your platform. To install, run:\r\n305: \r\n306:   ./sales-report-skill/install.sh\r\n307: \r\n308: Or specify your platform:\r\n309: \r\n310:   ./sales-report-skill/install.sh --platform cursor\r\n311: \r\n312: Or install to all detected platforms at once:\r\n313: \r\n314:   ./sales-report-skill/install.sh --all\r\n315: \r\n316: Alternative (if npx is available):\r\n317: \r\n318:   npx skills add ./sales-report-skill\r\n319: ```\r\n320: \r\n321: The `install.sh` inside the skill handles auto-detection, platform-specific paths, project vs user level, dry-run mode, and post-install activation instructions. It is the fallback for users who receive the skill as a package (not created in their current session).\r\n322: \r\n323: The generated skill must be a self-contained package that anyone can install with `git clone` or `./install.sh` and invoke with `/skill-name` — the same way agent-skill-creator itself works.\r\n324: \r\n325: ### Share With Your Team (Post-Creation)\r\n326: \r\n327: After installing the skill locally, always ask:\r\n328: \r\n329: ```\r\n330: Want to share this skill with your team so they can install it too?\r\n331: ```\r\n332: \r\n333: Corporate users don't know what a registry is, how to `git push`, or what `skill_registry.py` does. They just want their colleague to have the same skill. You handle everything.\r\n334: \r\n335: **If the user says yes, do all of this automatically:**\r\n336: \r\n337: 1. **Initialize a git repo** inside the generated skill directory:\r\n338:    ```bash\r\n339:    cd ./sales-report-skill\r\n340:    git init\r\n341:    git add -A\r\n342:    git commit -m \"feat: Initial skill — sales-report-skill\"\r\n343:    ```\r\n344: \r\n345: 2. **Detect the team's git platform** and create a remote repo:\r\n346: \r\n347:    Check which CLI tools are available and authenticated:\r\n348: \r\n349:    ```\r\n350:    gh auth status    → GitHub (github.com or GitHub Enterprise)\r\n351:    glab auth status  → GitLab (gitlab.com or self-hosted)\r\n352:    ```\r\n353: \r\n354:    **If `gh` is available (GitHub):**\r\n355:    ```bash\r\n356:    gh repo create sales-report-skill --public --source=. --push\r\n357:    gh repo edit --add-topic agent-skill\r\n358:    ```\r\n359: \r\n360:    **If `glab` is available (GitLab):**\r\n361:    ```bash\r\n362:    glab repo create sales-report-skill --public --defaultBranch main\r\n363:    git remote add origin <returned-url>\r\n364:    git push -u origin main\r\n365:    glab repo edit --topic agent-skill\r\n366:    ```\r\n367: \r\n368:    The `agent-skill` topic makes skills discoverable across the org. Teams can search `topic:agent-skill` on GitHub or filter by topic on GitLab to find all shared skills.\r\n369: \r\n370:    **If both are available**, check the existing git remotes in the current project to infer which platform the team uses. If the current project's `origin` points to `gitlab.com` or a GitLab instance, use `glab`. Otherwise default to `gh`.\r\n371: \r\n372:    **If neither is available**, tell the user:\r\n373:    ```\r\n374:    I can't create the repo automatically. To share this skill:\r\n375:    1. Create a new repo on GitHub or GitLab called \"sales-report-skill\"\r\n376:    2. Then run:\r\n377:       git remote add origin <repo-url>\r\n378:       git push -u origin main\r\n379:    3. Share the git clone link with your team\r\n380:    ```\r\n381: \r\n382: 3. **Give the user a shareable one-liner** they can send to colleagues:\r\n383:    ```\r\n384:    Shared! Your colleagues can install it by pasting this in their terminal:\r\n385: \r\n386:      git clone <repo-url> ~/.claude/skills/sales-report-skill\r\n387: \r\n388:    Or for VS Code Copilot:\r\n389: \r\n390:      git clone <repo-url> .github/skills/sales-report-skill\r\n391: \r\n392:    Or for Cursor:\r\n393: \r\n394:      git clone <repo-url> .cursor/rules/sales-report-skill\r\n395:    ```\r\n396: \r\n397:    Use the actual repo URL from step 2 (GitHub or GitLab). The install pattern is identical regardless of git platform.\r\n398: \r\n399: 4. **Optionally publish to the team registry** (if the agent-skill-creator registry is available):\r\n400:    ```bash\r\n401:    python3 scripts/skill_registry.py publish ./sales-report-skill/ --tags <auto-generated-tags>\r\n402:    ```\r\n403: \r\n404: The goal: the user who created the skill sends a one-liner to their colleague on Slack or Teams. The colleague pastes it. Done. No registry knowledge, no `skill_registry.py`, no understanding of the spec. Just `git clone` and it works — whether the team uses GitHub or GitLab.\r\n405: \r\n406: **If the user says no**, that's fine — the skill is already installed locally and working. They can always share later.\r\n407: \r\n408: ### Set Up a Team Skill Registry\r\n409: \r\n410: When a user mentions a team, organization, or colleagues — or when they ask about sharing skills at scale — offer to create a **team skill registry**. This is a shared git repo that acts as the central catalog where all team members publish and install skills.\r\n411: \r\n412: This is the model for AI consultants enabling corporate teams:\r\n413: 1. The consultant teaches each team member to install and use agent-skill-creator\r\n414: 2. The consultant creates one shared `{team}-skills-registry` repo on GitHub/GitLab\r\n415: 3. Each team member creates skills from their own workflows using `/agent-skill-creator`\r\n416: 4. Each member publishes to the shared registry\r\n417: 5. Other members browse, search, and install from that same registry\r\n418: \r\n419: The consultant delivers **knowledge and infrastructure**, not skills. The team creates the skills themselves — they know their workflows better than anyone.\r\n420: \r\n421: ```\r\n422: Want me to set up a shared skill registry for your team? It's a single\r\n423: repo where everyone publishes their skills and anyone can browse and\r\n424: install them — like an internal app store for agent skills.\r\n425: ```\r\n426: \r\n427: **If the user says yes, do all of this automatically:**\r\n428: \r\n429: 1. **Ask for the team or org name** to use in the registry name (e.g., \"engineering\", \"acme-corp\"):\r\n430: \r\n431: 2. **Initialize the registry**:\r\n432:    ```bash\r\n433:    mkdir -p ~/{team}-skills-registry\r\n434:    python3 scripts/skill_registry.py init --registry ~/{team}-skills-registry --name \"{Team Name} Skills\"\r\n435:    ```\r\n436: \r\n437: 3. **Create a remote repo** (same GitHub/GitLab detection as skill sharing):\r\n438:    ```bash\r\n439:    cd ~/{team}-skills-registry\r\n440:    git init && git add -A && git commit -m \"feat: Initialize {team} skill registry\"\r\n441: \r\n442:    # GitHub\r\n443:    gh repo create {team}-skills-registry --private --source=. --push\r\n444:    gh repo edit --add-topic agent-skill-registry\r\n445: \r\n446:    # Or GitLab\r\n447:    glab repo create {team}-skills-registry --private --defaultBranch main\r\n448:    git remote add origin <url> && git push -u origin main\r\n449:    ```\r\n450: \r\n451:    The registry repo should be **private** by default (internal to the org). The team admin controls who has access via GitHub/GitLab repo permissions.\r\n452: \r\n453: 4. **If a skill was just created**, publish it as the first entry:\r\n454:    ```bash\r\n455:    python3 scripts/skill_registry.py publish ./sales-report-skill/ --registry ~/{team}-skills-registry --tags sales,reports\r\n456:    cd ~/{team}-skills-registry && git add -A && git commit -m \"feat: Add sales-report-skill\" && git push\r\n457:    ```\r\n458: \r\n459: 5. **Give the user a team onboarding guide** they can share on Slack, Teams, or email:\r\n460: \r\n461:    ```\r\n462:    Registry is live! Share this with your team:\r\n463: \r\n464:    ──────────────────────────────────────────────\r\n465:    TEAM SKILL REGISTRY — Quick Start\r\n466:    ──────────────────────────────────────────────\r\n467: \r\n468:    STEP 1: Install agent-skill-creator (one time)\r\n469: \r\n470:      git clone https://github.com/FrancyJGLisboa/agent-skill-creator.git ~/.claude/skills/agent-skill-creator\r\n471: \r\n472:      For VS Code Copilot:\r\n473:        git clone https://github.com/FrancyJGLisboa/agent-skill-creator.git .github/skills/agent-skill-creator\r\n474: \r\n475:      For Cursor:\r\n476:        git clone https://github.com/FrancyJGLisboa/agent-skill-creator.git .cursor/rules/agent-skill-creator\r\n477: \r\n478:    STEP 2: Clone the team registry (one time)\r\n479: \r\n480:      git clone <registry-repo-url> ~/{team}-skills-registry\r\n481: \r\n482:    STEP 3: Create a skill from any workflow you do repeatedly\r\n483: \r\n484:      Open your IDE chat and type:\r\n485:        /agent-skill-creator Every week I pull sales data and generate a report\r\n486: \r\n487:    STEP 4: Publish your skill to the team registry\r\n488: \r\n489:      python3 scripts/skill_registry.py publish ./my-skill/ --registry ~/{team}-skills-registry --tags tag1,tag2\r\n490:      cd ~/{team}-skills-registry && git add -A && git commit -m \"Add my-skill\" && git push\r\n491: \r\n492:    STEP 5: Install a colleague's skill\r\n493: \r\n494:      git pull (inside ~/{team}-skills-registry to get latest)\r\n495:      python3 scripts/skill_registry.py list --registry ~/{team}-skills-registry\r\n496:      python3 scripts/skill_registry.py search \"sales\" --registry ~/{team}-skills-registry\r\n497:      python3 scripts/skill_registry.py install sales-report-skill --registry ~/{team}-skills-registry\r\n498: \r\n499:    ──────────────────────────────────────────────\r\n500:    ```\r\n501: \r\n502: **When to offer registry setup:**\r\n503: - User mentions \"team\", \"organization\", \"department\", \"colleagues\", \"company\"\r\n504: - User asks about sharing or distributing skills at scale\r\n505: - User is an AI consultant or admin setting up infrastructure for others\r\n506: \r\n507: **When NOT to offer:**\r\n508: - User is creating a single personal skill\r\n509: - A registry already exists (check for `~/team-skills-registry` or similar)\r\n510: \r\n511: The registry is a git repo. Version history, access control, and review workflows come free from GitHub/GitLab. No servers, no databases, no new tools.\r\n512: \r\n513: See `references/pipeline-phases.md` for detailed Phase 5 instructions.\r\n514: \r\n515: ### Update Check\r\n516: \r\n517: When loading this skill, silently check if the local copy is behind the remote. Run:\r\n518: \r\n519: ```bash\r\n520: cd <skill-install-dir> && git rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null\r\n521: cd <skill-install-dir> && git ls-remote origin HEAD 2>/dev/null | cut -f1\r\n522: ```\r\n523: \r\n524: If both commands succeed and the hashes differ, mention to the user:\r\n525: \"A newer version of agent-skill-creator is available. Run `git pull` in <path> to update.\"\r\n526: \r\n527: Do not block or interrupt for this. If either command fails (no git, no network, not a git repo), skip silently.\r\n528: \r\n529: ### Generated SKILL.md Format\r\n530: \r\n531: Every generated skill's SKILL.md must follow this structure:\r\n532: \r\n533: ```yaml\r\n534: ---\r\n535: name: skill-name-skill      # 1-64 chars, must end with -skill, matches directory\r\n536: description: >-             # 1-1024 chars, activation keywords\r\n537:   Description here...\r\n538: license: MIT                # or appropriate license\r\n539: metadata:\r\n540:   author: Author Name\r\n541:   version: 1.0.0\r\n542:   created: YYYY-MM-DD                # When the skill was created\r\n543:   last_reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD          # Last time content was verified current\r\n544:   review_interval_days: 90           # Days between required reviews\r\n545:   dependencies:                      # External URLs the skill depends on (optional)\r\n546:     - url: https://api.example.com/v1\r\n547:       name: Example API\r\n548:       type: api\r\n549:   schema_expectations:               # Expected API response shapes (optional)\r\n550:     - url: https://api.example.com/v1/data\r\n551:       method: GET\r\n552:       expected_keys:\r\n553:         - id\r\n554:         - name\r\n555:         - value\r\n556: ---\r\n557: # /skill-name — Short Description\r\n558: \r\n559: You are an expert [domain]. Your job is to [what the skill does].\r\n560: \r\n561: ## Trigger\r\n562: \r\n563: User invokes `/skill-name` followed by their input:\r\n564: \r\n565: [examples of invocation]\r\n566: \r\n567: ## [Rest of skill body — workflow, instructions, references]\r\n568: ```\r\n569: \r\n570: The SKILL.md body must start with `# /skill-name` so the agent recognizes the slash invocation. The body must be <500 lines. Move detailed content to `references/`.\r\n571: \r\n572: **Critical**: Every skill the factory produces must be invocable with `/skill-name` on any platform. The generated skill is software that gets installed and used — not a document to read.\r\n573: \r\n574: ## Architecture Decision\r\n575: \r\n576: | Factor | Simple Skill | Complex Suite |\r\n577: |--------|-------------|---------------|\r\n578: | Workflows | 1-2 | 3+ distinct |\r\n579: | Code size | <1000 lines | >2000 lines |\r\n580: | Maintenance | Single developer | Team |\r\n581: | Structure | Single SKILL.md | Multiple component SKILL.md files |\r\n582: | marketplace.json | Not needed | Optional (official fields only) |\r\n583: \r\n584: See `references/architecture-guide.md` for detailed decision framework.\r\n585: \r\n586: ## Cross-Platform Support\r\n587: \r\n588: Generated skills work across 17 tools in 3 tiers. Every generated skill outputs both **SKILL.md** (skill definition, ~15 tools) and **AGENTS.md** (instruction file, ~15 tools) to maximize reach.\r\n589: \r\n590: ### Tier 1 — Native SKILL.md (reads directly, no conversion)\r\n591: \r\n592: | Platform | Native Global Path | Native Project Path | Command |\r\n593: |----------|-------------------|--------------------|---------|\r\n594: | Claude Code | `~/.claude/skills/` | `.claude/skills/` | `./install.sh` |\r\n595: | GitHub Copilot | `~/.copilot/skills/` | `.github/skills/` | `./install.sh --platform copilot` |\r\n596: | Codex CLI | `~/.agents/skills/` | `.agents/skills/` | `./install.sh --platform codex` |\r\n597: | Gemini CLI | `~/.gemini/skills/` | `.gemini/skills/` | `./install.sh --platform gemini` |\r\n598: | Kiro | `~/.kiro/skills/` | `.kiro/skills/` | `./install.sh --platform kiro` |\r\n599: | Goose | `~/.config/goose/skills/` | — | `./install.sh --platform goose` |\r\n600: | OpenCode | `~/.config/opencode/skills/` | `.opencode/skills/` | `./install.sh --platform opencode` |\r\n601: | Cline | `~/.cline/skills/` | `.clinerules/skills/` | `./install.sh --platform cline` |\r\n602: | Roo Code | `~/.roo/skills/` | `.roo/skills/` | `./install.sh --platform roo-code` |\r\n603: | Kilo Code | `~/.kilocode/skills/` | `.kilocode/skills/` | `./install.sh --platform kilo-code` |\r\n604: | Factory Droid | `~/.factory/skills/` | `.factory/skills/` | `./install.sh --platform factory` |\r\n605: | Antigravity | — | `.agent/skills/` | `./install.sh --platform antigravity` |\r\n606: \r\n607: ### Tier 2 — Auto-adapted (installer converts SKILL.md to native format)\r\n608: \r\n609: | Platform | Native Format | Adaptation | Install Path | Command |\r\n610: |----------|--------------|------------|-------------|---------|\r\n611: | Cursor | `.mdc` | Generates `.mdc` with `alwaysApply`/`globs` frontmatter | `.cursor/skills/` (project only, no global) | `./install.sh --platform cursor` |\r\n612: | Windsurf | `.md` rules | Generates plain `.md` rule (6K char limit per file) | `.windsurf/rules/` (project) or `~/.codeium/windsurf/` (global) | `./install.sh --platform windsurf` |\r\n613: | Trae | `.md` rules | Generates plain `.md` with `type:` frontmatter | `.trae/rules/` | `./install.sh --platform trae` |\r\n614: | Junie | `guidelines.md` | Extracts body as plain markdown | `.junie/skills/` | `./install.sh --platform junie` |\r\n615: \r\n616: ### Tier 3 — Manual integration\r\n617: \r\n618: | Platform | Config File | Instructions |\r\n619: |----------|------------|-------------|\r\n620: | Zed | `.rules` | Copy SKILL.md body into `.rules` file |\r\n621: | Augment | `.augment/rules/` | Copy as `.md` with `type: Always` frontmatter |\r\n622: | Aider | `CONVENTIONS.md` | Copy SKILL.md body into CONVENTIONS.md |\r\n623: | Continue.dev | `.continue/rules/` | Copy as `.md` with Continue frontmatter |\r\n624: \r\n625: ### Companion AGENTS.md\r\n626: \r\n627: Every generated skill also outputs an `AGENTS.md` file alongside SKILL.md. This extends reach to tools that prioritize AGENTS.md over SKILL.md (Codex CLI, Augment, Continue.dev, Zed, and others). The AGENTS.md contains the skill's purpose, activation triggers, and usage instructions in the AAIF-governed format.\r\n628: \r\n629: See `references/cross-platform-guide.md` for full platform details.\r\n630: \r\n631: ## Validation and Security\r\n632: \r\n633: After generating a skill, run:\r\n634: \r\n635: - **Spec validation**: Checks frontmatter, naming, structure, line count\r\n636: - **Security scan**: Checks for hardcoded API keys, .env files, injection patterns\r\n637: \r\n638: ```bash\r\n639: # Validate a skill\r\n640: python3 scripts/validate.py path/to/skill/\r\n641: \r\n642: # Security scan\r\n643: python3 scripts/security_scan.py path/to/skill/\r\n644: ```\r\n645: \r\n646: ## Export System\r\n647: \r\n648: Package skills for distribution:\r\n649: \r\n650: ```bash\r\n651: # Export for all platforms\r\n652: python3 scripts/export_utils.py path/to/skill/\r\n653: \r\n654: # Desktop/Web package only\r\n655: python3 scripts/export_utils.py path/to/skill/ --variant desktop\r\n656: \r\n657: # API package only\r\n658: python3 scripts/export_utils.py path/to/skill/ --variant api\r\n659: ```\r\n660: \r\n661: See `references/export-guide.md` for full export documentation.\r\n662: \r\n663: ## Template-Based Creation\r\n664: \r\n665: Pre-built templates for common domains:\r\n666: \r\n667: - **Financial Analysis**: Alpha Vantage/Yahoo Finance, fundamental + technical analysis\r\n668: - **Climate Analysis**: Open-Meteo/NOAA, anomalies + trends + seasonal patterns\r\n669: - **E-commerce Analytics**: Google Analytics/Stripe/Shopify, traffic + revenue + cohorts\r\n670: \r\n671: See `references/templates-guide.md` for template details and customization.\r\n672: \r\n673: ## Multi-Agent Suites\r\n674: \r\n675: Create multiple related agents in one operation:\r\n676: \r\n677: ```\r\n678: \"Create a financial analysis suite with 4 agents:\r\n679: fundamental, technical, portfolio, and risk assessment\"\r\n680: ```\r\n681: \r\n682: See `references/multi-agent-guide.md` for suite creation docs.\r\n683: \r\n684: ## Interactive Configuration\r\n685: \r\n686: Step-by-step wizard for complex projects:\r\n687: \r\n688: ```\r\n689: \"Help me create an agent with interactive options\"\r\n690: \"Walk me through creating a financial analysis system\"\r\n691: ```\r\n692: \r\n693: See `references/interactive-mode.md` for wizard documentation.\r\n694: \r\n695: ## AgentDB Integration\r\n696: \r\n697: Optional learning system that gets smarter over time:\r\n698: \r\n699: - Stores creation episodes for pattern learning\r\n700: - Progressively improves API selection, architecture, and keywords\r\n701: - Works identically with or without AgentDB available\r\n702: \r\n703: See `references/agentdb-integration.md` for integration details.\r\n704: \r\n705: ## Quality Standards\r\n706: \r\n707: **Always**:\r\n708: - Complete, functional code (no TODOs, no `pass`)\r\n709: - Detailed docstrings and type hints\r\n710: - Robust error handling\r\n711: - Real content in references (not \"see docs\")\r\n712: - Configs with real values\r\n713: \r\n714: **Never**:\r\n715: - Placeholder code or empty functions\r\n716: - `api_key: YOUR_KEY_HERE` without env var instructions\r\n717: - SKILL.md over 500 lines\r\n718: - Platform-specific hacks\r\n719: \r\n720: See `references/quality-standards.md` for complete standards.\r\n721: \r\n722: ## Naming Convention\r\n723: \r\n724: Every generated skill name must end with `-skill`. This suffix makes skills instantly discoverable across GitHub and GitLab organizations — teams can search `*-skill` and find every skill in their org.\r\n725: \r\n726: **Format**: `{domain}-{objective}-skill`\r\n727: \r\n728: **Rules**:\r\n729: - Must end with `-skill`\r\n730: - 1-64 characters total, lowercase letters, numbers, and hyphens\r\n731: - Must match parent directory name\r\n732: - Must not contain consecutive hyphens\r\n733: \r\n734: **Examples**: `sales-report-skill`, `csv-cleaner-skill`, `deploy-checklist-skill`, `stock-analyzer-skill`\r\n735: \r\n736: **Suites**: `{domain}-suite` (suites are not suffixed with `-skill` — they contain skills)\r\n737: \r\n738: The `-skill` suffix also serves as a signal to the agent: when it sees a repo or directory ending in `-skill`, it knows this is installable, invocable software — not documentation or a regular project.\r\n739: \r\n740: ## Reference Files\r\n741: \r\n742: | File | Contents |\r\n743: |------|----------|\r\n744: | `references/pipeline-phases.md` | Detailed Phase 1-5 instructions |\r\n745: | `references/architecture-guide.md` | Simple vs Suite decision, refactoring, cross-component communication, versioning |\r\n746: | `references/templates-guide.md` | Template-based creation |\r\n747: | `references/interactive-mode.md` | Interactive wizard docs |\r\n748: | `references/multi-agent-guide.md` | Suite creation, orchestration patterns, routing logic |\r\n749: | `references/agentdb-integration.md` | AgentDB learning system |\r\n750: | `references/cross-platform-guide.md` | Platform compatibility matrix |\r\n751: | `references/export-guide.md` | Cross-platform export system |\r\n752: | `references/quality-standards.md` | Quality standards, dependency management, testing strategy |\r\n753: | `references/phase4-detection.md` | Detection & keyword-design craft reference |\r\n754: | `references/phase2-eval-assessment.md` | Phase 2 eval-criteria step, golden-case strategy, spec format, autoresearch handoff |\r\n755: | `references/phase5-orchestration.md` | Phase 5 pipeline orchestration: single run_pipeline.py entry-point, deterministic sequencing, check_pipeline.py |\r\n756: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
